    "number": 39,
    "title": "JSON Web Tokens",
    "intro": "Now we are going to understand the token used by the next two lessons. We will examine the header, payload, and signature, then separate the login request that issues a token from later requests that present it.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What does a JWT represent here?",
        "paragraphs": [
          "It is a signed token carrying claims used by the application. Reading its payload is different from verifying the signature and deciding whether the token is valid."
        ],
        "flow": [
          "Credentials at login",
          "Authenticate user",
          "Issue signed JWT",
          "Send Bearer token",
          "Validate later request"
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Why issue a token?",
        "paragraphs": [
          "In the office analogy, repeatedly checking an employee against a register is replaced by issuing an access card after the first identity check. The employee then presents the card when accessing the office.",
          "The lesson relates this to sending credentials for the initial authenticate request and then sending a token for subsequent requests. It discusses multiple office/application locations and the need to validate access across them."
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "What the token carries",
        "paragraphs": [
          "JWT means JSON Web Token. The lesson contrasts a compact JSON-based token with a bulkier XML representation for information sent in a web request.",
          "The client holds the issued token. The illustrated validation flow does not need a stored server-side record of that token. This is the stateless-token point made in the diagram, not a claim that the application stores no users or never performs another database lookup."
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "The three-part format",
        "code": {
          "title": "Token layout — symbolic format",
          "lang": "text",
          "text": "header.payload.signature"
        },
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Part",
            "Information explained"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Header",
              "Token/algorithm information; the example selects HS256."
            ],
            [
              "Payload",
              "User or identity information and claims such as issue time, expiration, or role information."
            ],
            [
              "Signature",
              "The part checked using the signing information when validating the token."
            ]
          ]
        },
        "paragraphs": [
          "The token viewer shows the three parts separated by dots. The lesson changes the algorithm selection and examines the decoded information. It does not implement a separate token-encryption scheme."
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "First request: authenticate and issue",
        "steps": [
          "Send username and password to a login/authenticate endpoint.",
          "Validate those credentials.",
          "Create a token for the authenticated user.",
          "Return it to the client."
        ],
        "paragraphs": [
          "The implementation of that flow comes in the next lesson. No second login endpoint or separate access-card project is added here."
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Later requests: send and validate",
        "code": {
          "title": "Header format discussed",
          "lang": "text",
          "text": "Authorization: Bearer <issued-token>"
        },
        "steps": [
          "Read the token from the Authorization header.",
          "Extract and parse its information.",
          "Verify its signature.",
          "Check the expiration.",
          "Allow the request to reach the application operation when validation succeeds."
        ],
        "paragraphs": [
          "Postman’s Bearer Token option places it in this header. If validation fails or the token has expired, the diagram returns a failure rather than processing the protected operation."
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Expiration and token possession",
        "paragraphs": [
          "The application chooses an expiration time. After expiration, the client must authenticate again to obtain a fresh token in the flow taught here.",
          "We explicitly notes that the client must protect its token: someone else who obtains it may be able to present it. Signature validation does not replace that possession concern. No extra client-storage or refresh-token design is introduced."
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Fit JWT into the security architecture",
        "paragraphs": [
          "The upcoming implementation adds a JWT utility and libraries for generation, parsing, signature validation, and expiration checks. Those functions connect the token flow to the existing security architecture. This lesson explains the format and flow; the next lessons implement issuance and validation."
        ]
      }
    ],
